/// A collection method called with too many arguments raises, catchably.
///
/// Issue #27.  The rule is symmetric: ALGOL-24.md's 'Expected 1 arguments but
/// got 2.' is the same sentence with the count the other way round, and a call
/// that supplies more than a method takes is as wrong as one that supplies
/// fewer.
///
/// ⚠️ Compiled, this does not crash and does not raise.  It SUCCEEDS -- exit 0,
/// the extra argument silently discarded, the call answering as though it had
/// been written correctly.  'collection_method' (bootstrap/algol.c:2345)
/// discards its count with '(void)count;' and reads only the arguments it
/// expects, so a surplus is invisible.
///
/// ⚠️ This is the quiet half of #27 and it is worth keeping separate from the
/// crash.  A fix that only stops the segfault -- checking that ENOUGH arguments
/// arrived before indexing args -- leaves this file failing, because too many
/// is not too few.  The guard has to compare the count both ways.  Split into
/// its own file so the harness says which half is fixed rather than reporting
/// the pair as one line.
///
/// Interpreted: passes.
/// Compiled:    the assertion fails -- nothing was raised, so Caught is empty.
///
test 'A Collection Method With Too Many Arguments Raises';
begin
    var L := [1, 2, 3];
    var Caught := '';

    try
        L.Get (0, 99);
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Expected 1 arguments but got 2.', Caught);
end
